<%- include('../partials/header') %>

<div class="page-head">
  <div class="titleblock">
    <p class="eyebrow">Manage</p>
    <h1>Import photographs</h1>
    <p class="sub">
      The parish already has the pictures, one per household in a folder. Name
      each file after the family it belongs to, zip the folder, and upload it
      here once, instead of visiting two hundred pages.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<%
  /*
   * The outcome, above everything, for the reason the spreadsheet import puts
   * it there: it is the only thing the person is looking for when the page
   * comes back. Either `problems` or `result` is filled in, never both.
   */
%>
<% if (problems.length) { %>
  <div class="alert error">
    <strong>
      No photographs were imported<%= fileName ? ` from ${fileName}` : '' %>.
      <% if (problemCount > 1) { %>
        There are <%= problemCount %> files to fix first.
      <% } %>
    </strong>
    <ul>
      <% problems.forEach(function (problem) { %>
        <li><%= problem.message %></li>
      <% }); %>
      <% if (problemsHidden) { %>
        <li>&hellip;and <%= problemsHidden %> more. Fix these first &mdash;
          the same mistake usually explains the rest.</li>
      <% } %>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">
      Correct the folder, make the zip again, and upload it once more. Not one
      photograph was stored, so there is nothing to undo and no family has
      changed.
    </p>
  </div>
<% } %>

<% if (result) { %>
  <div class="alert notice">
    <%
      /*
       * One finished sentence, built in the route rather than assembled from
       * fragments here: EJS puts a line break wherever the template has one,
       * so a headline assembled from four tags across four lines arrives with
       * newlines inside the sentence.
       */
    %>
    <strong><%= result.headline %></strong>
    <p style="margin:8px 0 0">
      <%= withPhotos %> of this parish&rsquo;s <%= totalFamilies %>
      famil<%= totalFamilies === 1 ? 'y' : 'ies' %> now
      <%= withPhotos === 1 ? 'has' : 'have' %> a photograph. Nothing else about
      any family changed &mdash; a draft is still a draft, and a published
      entry is still published.
    </p>

    <% if (result.replacedFamilies.length) { %>
      <p style="margin:10px 0 0">
        Replaced the photograph already on record for:
        <% result.replacedFamilies.forEach(function (ref, i) { %><code><%= ref %></code><%= i < result.replacedFamilies.length - 1 ? ', ' : '' %><% }); %>.
      </p>
    <% } %>

    <% if (result.failed.length) { %>
      <p style="margin:10px 0 4px">
        These looked fine a moment earlier and then did not &mdash; somebody
        else was probably working on the directory at the same time:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <% result.failed.forEach(function (item) { %>
          <li><%= item.file %>: <%= item.message %></li>
        <% }); %>
      </ul>
    <% } %>
  </div>
<% } %>

<%
  /*
   * Two columns: everything to read on the left, the one thing to do on the
   * right, where it stays put while the instructions are scrolled.
   *
   * The upload panel is short and the instructions are long, so stacking them
   * the way this page used to put the file picker below a screen and a half of
   * prose — a person who knew the rules still had to scroll past all of them to
   * reach the control, every time they came back to correct a folder.
   *
   * `.cols` and `.side` are the dashboard's, and collapse to one column below
   * 1080px. On a narrow screen the order in the markup is the order on the
   * page: instructions first, then the upload, which is the right way round for
   * somebody meeting this page for the first time on a laptop or a tablet.
   *
   * The report above stays full width and above both columns. It is the only
   * thing the person is looking for when the page comes back after an upload.
   */
%>
<div class="cols">
  <div class="instructions">

<div class="card">
  <h2>Naming the files</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>Each file is named after the family&rsquo;s Family ID</strong>,
    the same reference the directory holds and the spreadsheet import groups
    by. Family <code>F-001</code> gets <code>F-001.jpg</code>. Nothing else
    about the name matters: letter case is ignored, so
    <code>f-001.JPG</code> is the same family.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    One photograph per family. Two files named for the same family, like
    <code>F-001.jpg</code> alongside <code>F-001.png</code>, stop the import
    rather than have this app guess which one the parish meant.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    Put them all in one folder, then right-click the folder and choose
    <strong>Send to &rarr; Compressed (zipped) folder</strong> on Windows, or
    <strong>Compress</strong> on a Mac. Folders inside the zip are fine, since
    only the file names are read.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <%= extensions.join(', ') %> are accepted, up to
    <%= maxPhotoMb %>&nbsp;MB each and <%= maxArchiveMb %>&nbsp;MB for the
    whole archive. Every photograph must be <strong>landscape</strong>, wider
    than it is tall. The printed directory sets them in a landscape frame, and
    a portrait picture would cut off the top of somebody&rsquo;s head.
  </p>
</div>

<%
  /*
   * What to ask the photographer for.
   *
   * The numbers are the printed frame, not a preference. The frame is 4:3 in
   * both books (.photo / .page-single .photo in directory.css) and it is
   * `object-fit: cover`, so a picture of any other shape is not letterboxed —
   * it is cropped to fill, and the parish does not get to choose which edge
   * goes. That is the part worth saying out loud, because it is invisible
   * until the book is printed.
   *
   * The two printed sizes are worked out in directory.css: 76×57mm in the
   * two-up book (see --photo-width) and about 127×95mm on the one-family
   * sheet (--single-photo, 68% of 186mm of content). 1200×900px covers the
   * first at 400dpi and the second at 240dpi, which is why "larger is welcome"
   * is here rather than a flat "300dpi is enough".
   *
   * None of this is enforced. The importer checks landscape and nothing else
   * (lib/import-photos.js), so this is a card of advice and says so — a page
   * that reads like a rule, and then imports a 400px photograph without
   * complaint, teaches people to stop reading the page.
   */
%>
<div class="card">
  <h2>Photo size and resolution</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>4&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;3 inches, landscape, 300&nbsp;dpi.</strong>
    In pixels, which is what a file will actually tell you, that is
    <strong>1200&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;900</strong>. Ask whoever takes or scans the
    photographs for that and the whole folder will print cleanly.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    The frame in the book is <strong>4:3</strong>, and a picture of any other
    shape is <strong>cropped to fill it</strong> rather than shrunk to fit
    inside it &mdash; a very wide photograph loses its sides, a nearly square
    one loses its top and bottom. Framing a family at 4&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;3
    before uploading is how the parish decides what stays in the picture
    instead of leaving it to the crop.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <strong>Larger is welcome</strong>, and costs nothing but the file size
    limit above. The two-up directory prints each photograph at about
    76&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;57&nbsp;mm, but a single family printed on its own
    sheet gets about 127&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;95&nbsp;mm &mdash; roughly
    5&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;3&frac34; inches &mdash; and that is the page where a
    small photograph starts to look soft.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    This is a recommendation, not a rule the import enforces. The only shape
    it insists on is landscape: a smaller or oddly proportioned picture is
    imported without complaint, and shows up when the book is printed rather
    than today.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>What the import will and will not do</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>All of the folder, or none of it.</strong> One bad file stops the
    whole archive, so an import that reports success has stored every
    photograph you sent it. You never end up with a half-loaded folder.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <strong>A family that already has a photograph gets the new one</strong>.
    The old file is removed once the new one is safely stored. Every
    replacement is listed by Family ID in the summary, so re-uploading a
    corrected folder is a normal, visible thing to do.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <strong>Nothing else about a family changes.</strong> No entry is
    published, unpublished or edited, and no logins are created. A family with
    no photograph in the archive simply keeps whatever it had.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    Photographs can still be added or removed one at a time from each
    family&rsquo;s own page, which is where a single late picture belongs.
  </p>
</div>

  </div><%# /.instructions %>

  <div class="side">
    <form class="card upload-panel" method="post" action="<%= base %>/admin/photos"
          enctype="multipart/form-data">
      <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="<%= csrfToken %>">

      <h2>Upload the folder</h2>
      <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
        Every image in the archive is opened and checked before a single one is
        stored. If anything is wrong anywhere &mdash; a name that matches no
        family, a picture that is portrait, or a file that is not really the
        kind of image its name claims &mdash; it is listed at the top of this
        page in plain words, and <strong>nothing at all is imported</strong>.
        Fix the folder and upload it again.
      </p>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="archive">Your folder of photographs, zipped</label>
        <input type="file" id="archive" name="archive" accept=".zip,application/zip" required>
        <p class="help">
          A single <code>.zip</code> file. A large archive takes a moment to
          check &mdash; every picture in it is opened.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit" class="btn">Check and import</button>
      </div>
    </form>
  </div>
</div><%# /.cols %>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
